  "final_text": "All four steps are done.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Flights' reads from the `kibana_sample_data_flights` index. Its time field is `timestamp`. It has 33 fields, including `Carrier` (text), `Cancelled` (true/false), `FlightDelayMin`, `AvgTicketPrice`, `Dest` and `Origin`.\n2. **Dashboard:** I created 'S4-25800bd1 Flights' (id `s4-25800bd1-flights-003a5d21d325`) with exactly two panels:\n   - **Flights over time:** a line chart of the flight count by `timestamp`.\n   - **Flights per carrier:** a bar chart of the flight count for the top 10 values of `Carrier`.\n3. **Alert rule:** I created 'S4-25800bd1 Cancelled flights' (id `c3a2ef1b-ce9c-4603-ad18-038959ff96ed`) as an Elasticsearch query rule. It runs every minute, searches for flights where `Cancelled` is true, and fires when there are more than 10 in a 5-minute window. I created it already turned on, so there was no separate enable step.\n4. **Edit and check:**\n   - I renamed the carrier panel to 'Flights by carrier', and reading the dashboard back shows the new title.\n   - Reading the rule back shows it is enabled, with status `ok`.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
